\title{Logarithmic Lifts, Quantized Winding, and the Topological Charge of Orbital-Angular-Momentum Beams}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{15 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a self-contained analytic account of topological charge for complex optical fields sampled around a closed azimuthal contour. For a smooth non-vanishing loop $\gamma:[0,2\pi]\to\mathbb C\setminus\{0\}$, the charge is the logarithmic-derivative integral
\[
w(\gamma)=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_0^{2\pi}\frac{\gamma'(\theta)}{\gamma(\theta)}\,d\theta.
\]
We establish the product law, inversion and scaling laws, integer quantization, and realization of every integer by an orbital-angular-momentum phase. We characterize the kernel exactly: zero winding is equivalent to a logarithmic lift that closes after one turn. This yields a sharp dressing theorem and proves that the full off-axis Laguerre--Gauss-like amplitude carries the charge of its phase. For a model $(p,q)$ torus beam, the meridional charge is $pq$ and, for coprime positive parameters, equals $\lcm(p,q)$; the trefoil case has charge $6$. Numerical phase-increment algorithms and their stability conditions are also described.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
A complex optical field has local amplitude and phase. Around an optical vortex, its phase can accumulate an integral multiple of $2\pi$ although the field returns to the same value after one geometric circuit. This integer is the topological charge. It records handedness and multiplicity of the twist and supplies the orbital-angular-momentum label of structured light.

The correct general object is a loop in the punctured complex plane. The exclusion of zero is decisive: phase is undefined at a zero, and winding can change only by crossing that point. The logarithmic derivative $\gamma'/\gamma$ captures infinitesimal changes, while its contour integral isolates the global phase defect. The product rule, quantization theorem, and logarithmic-lift kernel theorem answer three questions: why charges add under multiplication, which dressings preserve charge, and what distinguishes zero charge analytically.

\section{Definitions and optical models}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{smooth non-vanishing closed loop} is a continuously differentiable map
$\gamma:[0,2\pi]\to\mathbb C\setminus\{0\}$ satisfying $\gamma(2\pi)=\gamma(0)$. Its logarithmic derivative is
\[
D_{\log}\gamma(\theta)=\frac{\gamma'(\theta)}{\gamma(\theta)},
\]
and its \emph{winding number} or \emph{topological charge} is
\[
w(\gamma)=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_0^{2\pi}D_{\log}\gamma(\theta)\,d\theta.
\]
\end{definition}

For $\ell\in\mathbb Z$, the elementary orbital-angular-momentum phase and a Laguerre--Gauss-like amplitude are
\[
\Phi_\ell(\theta)=e^{i\ell\theta},\qquad
A_\ell(r,\theta)=r^{|\ell|}\Phi_\ell(\theta).
\]
When $\ell\ne0$, the latter vanishes at $r=0$ and is nonzero at every $r>0$.

\begin{definition}
A \emph{periodic logarithmic lift} of $\gamma$ is a differentiable function $L:[0,2\pi]\to\mathbb C$ such that
\[
e^{L(\theta)}=\gamma(\theta),\qquad
L'(\theta)=\frac{\gamma'(\theta)}{\gamma(\theta)},\qquad
L(2\pi)=L(0).
\]
\end{definition}
The final requirement says that the logarithm itself, rather than merely its exponential, returns to its starting value.

\section{Algebra of winding}

\begin{theorem}[Product law]
Let $\gamma$ and $\delta$ be continuously differentiable, non-vanishing loops with continuous logarithmic derivatives. Then
\[
w(\gamma\delta)=w(\gamma)+w(\delta).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The Leibniz rule and non-vanishing give
\[
\frac{(\gamma\delta)'}{\gamma\delta}
=\frac{\gamma'\delta+\gamma\delta'}{\gamma\delta}
=\frac{\gamma'}{\gamma}+\frac{\delta'}{\delta}.
\]
Integrate and use linearity. Multiplication by $(2\pi i)^{-1}$ proves the result.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Identity, inversion, and powers]
For every admissible loop $\gamma$ and every $m\in\mathbb Z$,
\[
w(1)=0,\qquad w(\gamma^{-1})=-w(\gamma),\qquad
w(\gamma^m)=m w(\gamma).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
A constant has derivative zero. Apply the product law to $\gamma\gamma^{-1}=1$, then use induction for positive powers and inversion for negative powers.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Constant scaling]
If $c\in\mathbb C\setminus\{0\}$, then $w(c\gamma)=w(\gamma)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The constant loop $c$ has zero winding, so the product law applies. Equivalently, $(c\gamma)'/(c\gamma)=\gamma'/\gamma$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Winding is not additive under pointwise addition of fields. For example, $\Phi_1+\Phi_1=2\Phi_1$ has winding $1$, not $2$. Addition can create zeros through interference; multiplication is the source operation corresponding to addition of winding numbers.
\end{remark}

\section{Quantization and realization}

\begin{theorem}[Elementary phase charge]
For every $\ell\in\mathbb Z$,
\[
w(\Phi_\ell)=\ell.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $\Phi_\ell'=i\ell\Phi_\ell$, its logarithmic derivative is $i\ell$. Thus
\[
w(\Phi_\ell)=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_0^{2\pi}i\ell\,d\theta=\ell.
\]
Closure follows from $e^{2\pi i\ell}=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Integer quantization]
For every smooth non-vanishing closed loop $\gamma$, there is $n\in\mathbb Z$ such that $w(\gamma)=n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set
\[
G(\theta)=\int_0^\theta\frac{\gamma'(t)}{\gamma(t)}\,dt.
\]
Differentiating $\gamma(\theta)e^{-G(\theta)}$ shows it is constant. Closure of $\gamma$ implies $e^{-G(2\pi)}=1$. The kernel of the complex exponential is $2\pi i\mathbb Z$, so $G(2\pi)=2\pi i n$ for an integer $n$. Division by $2\pi i$ gives $w(\gamma)=n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Surjectivity]
Every integer occurs as the winding number of a smooth non-vanishing closed loop.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
For $n\in\mathbb Z$, choose $\Phi_n(\theta)=e^{in\theta}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Finite-family conservation]
For $\ell_1,\ldots,\ell_N\in\mathbb Z$,
\[
\prod_{j=1}^N\Phi_{\ell_j}(\theta)
=\Phi_{\sum_{j=1}^N\ell_j}(\theta),\qquad
w\!\left(\prod_{j=1}^N\Phi_{\ell_j}\right)=\sum_{j=1}^N\ell_j.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Use $e^ae^b=e^{a+b}$ and apply the product law repeatedly.
\end{proof}
For opposite charges, $\Phi_\ell\Phi_{-\ell}=1$, so the product is non-vanishing and has zero winding.

\section{The logarithmic-lift kernel}

\begin{lemma}[Periodic logarithms have zero winding]
If $\gamma=e^L$ and $L(2\pi)=L(0)$, then $w(\gamma)=0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The logarithmic derivative of $e^L$ is $L'$. Hence
\[
w(\gamma)=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_0^{2\pi}L'(\theta)\,d\theta
=\frac{L(2\pi)-L(0)}{2\pi i}=0.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Constructing a closing logarithm]
Let $\gamma$ be smooth and non-vanishing. If $w(\gamma)=0$, then
\[
L(\theta)=\log\gamma(0)+\int_0^\theta\frac{\gamma'(t)}{\gamma(t)}\,dt
\]
is a periodic logarithmic lift, where any logarithm of the nonzero initial value may be chosen.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The fundamental theorem of calculus gives $L'=\gamma'/\gamma$. Set $R=e^L/\gamma$. The quotient rule gives $R'=0$. At zero, $R(0)=1$, so $R\equiv1$ and $e^L=\gamma$. Finally,
\[
L(2\pi)-L(0)=\int_0^{2\pi}\frac{\gamma'}{\gamma}\,d\theta
=2\pi i\,w(\gamma)=0.
\]
Thus the lift closes.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Logarithmic-lift kernel theorem]
For a smooth non-vanishing loop $\gamma$, the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $w(\gamma)=0$;
\item $\gamma$ admits a periodic logarithmic lift.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The two implications are the preceding lemmas.
\end{proof}
This theorem identifies the exact kernel of the multiplicative winding map: a loop is topologically trivial relative to the origin exactly when its logarithm can be chosen continuously and single-valuedly for a complete turn.

\section{Physical amplitude and dressing}

\begin{theorem}[Full off-axis amplitude]
For $\ell\in\mathbb Z$ and $r>0$,
\[
w\bigl(A_\ell(r,\cdot)\bigr)=\ell.
\]
If $\ell\ne0$, the amplitude vanishes on the axis and is nonzero at every positive radius.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
At fixed $r>0$, the factor $r^{|\ell|}$ is a nonzero constant. Constant-scaling invariance and the elementary phase theorem give the winding identity. At $r=0$, $0^{|\ell|}=0$ when $|\ell|>0$; for $r>0$, both factors are nonzero.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Periodic exponential dressing]
Let $L$ be differentiable with continuous derivative and $L(2\pi)=L(0)$. Then
\[
w(e^L)=0,
\qquad
w(e^L\gamma)=w(\gamma)
\]
for every smooth non-vanishing loop $\gamma$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first identity follows by integrating $L'$. The second is the product law.
\end{proof}

The topological condition is sharp. The envelope $e^{im\theta}$ is smooth, closed, and non-vanishing for integral $m$, but has winding $m$. Its logarithmic lift $im\theta$ has endpoint defect $2\pi im$ and fails to close unless $m=0$. By contrast,
\[
E(\theta)=e^{a\cos\theta+ib\sin\theta}
\]
varies in magnitude and phase but has the periodic logarithm $a\cos\theta+ib\sin\theta$, hence zero winding.

\section{Torus-beam arithmetic}
For integers $p,q$, define the model meridional phase
\[
T_{p,q}(\theta)=e^{ipq\theta}.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Torus-beam charge]
For all integers $p,q$,
\[
w(T_{p,q})=pq.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the elementary phase theorem with $\ell=pq$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Coprime torus-beam bridge]
If $p,q\in\mathbb N$ are coprime, then
\[
w(T_{p,q})=\lcm(p,q).
\]
For the trefoil parameters $(p,q)=(2,3)$, the charge is $6$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The identity $\gcd(p,q)\lcm(p,q)=pq$ and coprimality give $\lcm(p,q)=pq$. Combine this with the preceding theorem.
\end{proof}

Coprimality is essential: $(2,4)$ has model charge $8$, whereas $\lcm(2,4)=4$. The arithmetic identity reflects the distinction between a single torus knot and a multi-component torus link, but a componentwise optical theorem requires further analysis.

\section{Numerical algorithms}
Let $z_0,\ldots,z_{N-1}$ be nonzero samples in cyclic order and set $z_N=z_0$. Define
\[
\Delta_j=\Arg(z_{j+1}\overline{z_j})\in(-\pi,\pi],\qquad
\widehat w_N=\frac{1}{2\pi}\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}\Delta_j.
\]
If every true phase increment between neighboring samples has magnitude less than $\pi$, the principal branch selects the correct increment and the sum recovers the winding. The procedure takes $O(N)$ time and $O(1)$ auxiliary memory when streamed.

For sampled loops $z_j$ and $u_j$, a product-law diagnostic computes
\[
\varepsilon=\widehat w(zu)-\widehat w(z)-\widehat w(u).
\]
A large residual signals branch aliasing, under-resolution, or contamination near a zero. The endpoint defect of a lifted logarithm is approximated by
\[
D_N=i\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}\Delta_j\approx2\pi i\widehat w_N.
\]
Closure telescopes the log-magnitude changes, so a near-zero defect indicates a closing logarithm.

Every implementation should report $\rho_{\min}=\min_j|z_j|$ and the largest phase step. Samples near zero make phase unstable; increments near $\pi$ are ambiguous. Refinement is required until the estimated integer and diagnostics stabilize.

\section{Worked examples and applications}
Take $\gamma=e^{3i\theta}$ and $\delta=e^{-i\theta}$. Their windings are $3$ and $-1$, and their product $e^{2i\theta}$ has winding $2$. A family with charges $4,-2,-2,5$ multiplies to $e^{5i\theta}$ and has total charge $5$.

Dressing $\Phi_3$ by
\[
E_0(\theta)=\exp(0.4\cos\theta+0.2i\sin\theta)
\]
leaves winding equal to $3$, since the displayed exponent is periodic. Dressing instead by $E_{-2}=e^{-2i\theta}$ shifts the winding to $1$. The latter envelope is nowhere zero but not neutral.

For $a>0$, the distorted loop $\Gamma_{a,n}=e^{a\cos\theta}e^{in\theta}$ has winding $n$. Its distance from zero changes substantially, yet the radial factor has a periodic real logarithm.

In structured-light communication, integer charge can label spatial channels; the dressing theorem identifies amplitude changes that preserve a label. In optical manipulation, sign encodes handedness and winding magnitude records phase circulation. In beam characterization, the discrete estimator extracts charge from contour samples and is immune to a common nonzero calibration factor. For knotted singularities, the torus-beam theorem suggests a future componentwise charge accounting governed by $\gcd(p,q)\lcm(p,q)=pq$.

\section{Discussion and scope}
The logarithmic derivative unifies the theory: algebraically it turns products into sums, analytically its integral is a logarithmic endpoint difference, and topologically that defect counts circuits around the excluded origin. Robustness is therefore precise rather than unconditional. A varying nonzero envelope is neutral exactly when it has zero winding, equivalently under the stated smoothness assumptions when it admits a periodic logarithmic lift.

The contour must not pass through a field zero. Zeros inside it are allowed and are detected collectively by winding. The radial amplitude result concerns the stated model at fixed positive radius. The torus result concerns the meridional phase $e^{ipq\theta}$ and does not by itself establish the full three-dimensional singular geometry. The product theorem governs pointwise multiplication, not coherent addition; additive interference can create zeros and needs separate analysis.

Piecewise-smooth contours may be treated by splitting the interval and summing, provided non-vanishing and endpoint closure persist. Numerical phase unwrapping is reliable only with amplitude bounded away from zero and sufficient angular resolution.

\section{Future directions}
The logarithmic endpoint defect should be an integral multiple of $2\pi i$, independent of lift, and should classify loops up to non-vanishing homotopy. A general envelope should shift charge by exactly its own winding, with invariance precisely for envelopes in the periodic-logarithm kernel.

For a $(p,q)$ torus singularity, a natural conjecture is that $\gcd(p,q)$ counts connected components and each component carries meridional charge $\lcm(p,q)$, distributing total charge according to
\[
\gcd(p,q)\lcm(p,q)=pq.
\]
A uniformly non-vanishing parameterized family should have constant charge; a jump should force a zero and equal the signed local index of the singular events. Finally, for invertible matrix-valued polarization loops, determinant winding is a natural additive charge, with a kernel expected to be related to periodic matrix logarithms.

\section{Conclusion}
Topological charge is the winding of a complex optical field around zero. Its logarithmic-derivative integral proves multiplication additivity, integer quantization, and realization of every integer. The zero class consists exactly of loops with periodic logarithmic lifts, yielding a sharp dressing-invariance theorem and confirming that the full off-axis Laguerre--Gauss-like amplitude carries the phase charge. For coprime torus parameters, the meridional charge equals their least common multiple. Optical twisting, complex analysis, topology, and arithmetic are thereby organized by one invariant.

